Description: The Dawson et al. (2021) <doi:10.1021/acs.est.0c06117>
              quantiative structure-property relationship (QSPR) models make
              chemical-specific predictions of parameters needed for HTTK
              (high throughput toxicokinetics). Two parameters, typically measured
              in vitro, are predicted: CLint and fup.
              CLint is the intrinsic hepatic clearance (uL/min/million hepatocytes) from a
              pooled donor suspended hepatocyte incubation in vitro assay. Clint is
              correlated with the rate of metabolic clearance of a
              chemical by the liver. Dawson et al. (2021) predicts CLint using a categorical
              (slow / moderate /fast) random forest model. Fup is the chemical fraction
              unbound in plasma as measured in vitro. Fup characterize the extent to which
              the chemical binds to proteins and other plasma constituents. Higher binding
              can reduce the amount of chemical available for clearance. Dawson et al. (2021)
              predicts Fup using a regression (continuous) random forest model. Both QSPR
              models were evaluated as part of Wambaugh et al. (2026) <doi:10.1016/j.tiv.2025.106150>
